Moral possibility

    - it was past credence in a divine creator that instilled our present sense of morality,
      an age-old consensus that dissolved and left intact a deontic sense of right behaviour
      informed by a concept of moral law
        : ad `our` : the West’s
    - now, seeing our past credence justified by new evidence, I consider whether the same
      evidence justifies also a [renewed|{renewal of}] belief in the reality of moral law
        : re `credence.+justified by.+evidence` see `^*\(15\) belief.+would be justified` @
          ../20.evident_creator.brec
        !! only in a disjunction of creators (mundane ∨ supernatural) is credence justified
            : see `^*\(15\) belief in the disjunction of the two.+would be justified` @
              ../20.evident_creator.brec
            + screw the rhetorical adornment and go straight into the unadorned argument
                : re `(screw.+adornment) (and go.+to the.+argument)` join `${1}.+${2}$`s @
                  02.wrong_rhetoric/wrong_rhetoric.README.brec : here as there,
                  a wrong order of work is the basic fault
                - the next major task for the argument is to infer a practical principle of pure logic
                  that arguably (as I shall argue later) is enforced and thereby absolute
                    : c.f. `^*\+ argue for.+a stable ground of maintenance re practical logic$` @
                      notes.brec
                + state the premises that directly support that inference
